667604538,2026-10-28 20:48:01,topstockalerts,2017-02-19,110081,1,,"CNBC’s Jim Cramer said major technology companies are better positioned to withstand market turbulence thanks to their dominant market positions, strong profitability and AI investments. He identified Alphabet, Amazon, Apple, Meta, Microsoft, Nvidia, Tesla, SpaceX, Anthropic and OpenAI as businesses capable of weathering economic pressures, although inflation, rising oil prices and potential Fed rate hikes remain risks. Microsoft stood out after beating earnings and revenue expectations, with revenue up 19% and Azure growth accelerating to 48% from 42% in the previous quarter. Shares rose 4% after hours, while capital expenditures and finance leases of $45.2 billion came in below forecasts despite heavy AI infrastructure spending. Cramer called Microsoft the winner among the two major Magnificent Seven earnings reports. Cramer warned of possible analyst downgrades but emphasized that dominant technology companies remain too influential for investors to ignore. $AMZN $NVDA $TSLA $SPCX"
667604593,2026-10-28 20:49:04,topstockalerts,2017-02-19,110081,0,,"The U.S. Department of Justice is investigating Nvidia’s $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, seeking information on how the deal could affect competition in the AI industry. The acquisition, announced in September, is Nvidia’s second-largest purchase after its $20 billion acquisition of Groq assets, which has also attracted DOJ scrutiny. Hugging Face operates a major open-source AI platform used by more than 18 million people and 200,000 companies to share over 3 million models and 500,000 datasets. It also sells infrastructure and collaboration tools to organizations developing proprietary AI, including chipmakers. Regulators may be examining whether Nvidia’s ownership could give it an advantage over competing AI developers and hardware providers. The deal resembles Microsoft’s $7.5 billion acquisition of GitHub in 2018, highlighting the strategic value of platforms central to software and AI development. $NVDA"
